---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeMorse from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeMorse.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="nato-phonetic-alphabet-translator">
  <DevEncodeMorse client:load mode="nato" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why a spelling alphabet exists</h2>
    <p>
      Over a phone line, a radio or in a noisy room, whole groups of letters sound the same. B, C, D,
      E, G, P, T and V share the same vowel sound. F and S are almost identical without the high
      frequencies. M and N are impossible to tell apart. A booking reference read letter by letter often
      fails, and nobody notices: both sides think they agreed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The NATO phonetic alphabet replaces each letter with a word that cannot be confused with any other
      word in the set, even when the sound is very poor. That is a stronger rule than &ldquo;starts with
      the right letter&rdquo;, so the list looks arbitrary.
    </p>

    <h2>Alfa, not Alpha. Juliett, not Juliet.</h2>
    <p>
      Two spellings in the ICAO list look like typos but are correct. Alfa avoids &ldquo;ph&rdquo;,
      which speakers of many languages do not read as an F sound. Juliett has a double T so that French
      speakers do not treat the final T as silent. Both were deliberate choices when the alphabet was
      standardised in 1956, and the input here accepts both spellings.
    </p>

    <h2>Aviation number pronunciation</h2>
    <p>
      Numbers get the same treatment in aviation and maritime use, for the same reason:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Digit</th><th>Spoken</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Tree</td><td>The &ldquo;th&rdquo; sound does not survive a radio</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Fower</td><td>Two clear syllables, unlike &ldquo;for&rdquo;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Fife</td><td>Distinguishes it from &ldquo;fire&rdquo;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>Niner</td><td>Avoids collision with the German &ldquo;nein&rdquo;</td></tr>
          <tr><td>.</td><td>Decimal</td><td>&ldquo;Point&rdquo; is easily lost</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Toggle it on in the tool to see your text spelled the way a controller would read it.</p>

    <h2>It is not the only spelling alphabet</h2>
    <p>
      UK and US police forces use their own lists, which differ enough to cause confusion. Financial
      call centres often use first names. If you read something critical to a person who may use a
      different list, say &ldquo;NATO alphabet&rdquo; first, or read both, as in &ldquo;B for
      Bravo&rdquo;, which always works.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Read a reference to a call centre: booking codes, policy numbers, case IDs.</li>
      <li>Dictate a licence plate or postcode to emergency services or a delivery driver.</li>
      <li>Read a serial number from hardware to a support engineer.</li>
      <li>Confirm an email address letter by letter, where one wrong character means the mail never arrives.</li>
      <li>Amateur radio, where it is standard practice for callsigns.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Decoding back to text</h2>
    <p>
      The swap button reverses the direction: paste a list of code words and get the original string.
      Words that are not in the ICAO alphabet come back as a question mark, not a guess, so you can see
      where a transcription failed.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
